## Adding a tool

Add a decorated function in `src/{{ package_name }}/tools.py` and restart the
client so it rediscovers tools:

```python
@mcp.tool(title="Word count")
@instrumented
def word_count(text: Annotated[str, Field(description="Text to count.")]) -> int:
    """Count whitespace-separated words."""
    return len(text.split())
```

For progress, logs, or elicitation, make the tool `async` and add a
`ctx: Context` parameter. The SDK injects it and hides it from the schema.
See `docs/adding-tools.md`.

{% endif %}
## Adding a provider

Implement `chat()` (and `aclose()`) from `agent/llm.py`, add it to the
`Settings.provider` literal, and extend `build_llm`. The loop does not change.

## Observability

Each run writes `traces/<timestamp>-<run-id>/` with `run.json`, `spans.jsonl`,
`events.jsonl`, and `summary.json`. Typical spans are `agent.run` >
`agent.step` > `llm.chat` and `mcp.tools/call`.

```bash
jq -c '{name, ms:.duration_ms, status}' traces/*/spans.jsonl
```

## Testing

```bash
uv run ruff check .
uv run ruff format --check src tests
uv run pytest -m "not integration"
uv run pytest
```

Model calls in tests use `FakeLLM`, so the fast suite needs no model and no
network. Integration tests launch a real MCP subprocess.

## Safety

Tool arguments are untrusted.{% if include_mcp_server %} The calculator parses
expressions with an AST allowlist instead of `eval`.{% endif %} If you add file
tools, resolve paths against a fixed workspace root and reject absolute paths
and traversal. The HTTP transport binds to loopback and has no authentication;
do not expose it publicly without auth and TLS.
